Kernir patch 0005: the 7zip writer encrypts every folder (file data and the encoded header) with 7zAES

Upstream: libarchive 3.8.10, libarchive/archive_write_set_format_7zip.c (struct coder, struct _7zip,
_7z_options, _7z_write_header, compress_out, _7z_close, make_streamsInfo, _7z_free). Needs
include/kernir_7zaes.h and libarchive/kernir_7zaes.c (CommonCrypto, part of libSystem).

New option encryption=aes256 (also AES256, AES-256; NULL turns it off; anything else is
ARCHIVE_FAILED "Unknown encryption name"). With it, the first archive_write_header without a
passphrase fails ARCHIVE_FATAL "Encryption needs passphrase", so nothing is ever written in
plaintext by mistake. Each folder becomes [7zAES, codec], the order 7-Zip writes: one bind pair
(InIndex 1 <- OutIndex 0), the packed stream feeds 7zAES, and CodersUnPackSize lists 7zAES's
output (the codec's unpadded packed size) before the codec's. compress_out() encrypts wbuff in
place (61440 bytes, a multiple of 16) before it reaches the temporary file, so encryption streams
through the existing buffer; a folder's last block is zero-padded and its packed size rounded up
to 16. Props: key cost 2^19, no salt, a fresh 16-byte IV per folder from CCRandomGenerateBytes.
The key is derived once per archive (no salt, so every folder shares it) and wiped with memset_s.
The header is always encoded when encrypting (upstream leaves a one-entry header unencoded), so
names are hidden as with 7-Zip's -mhe=on. With COPY the data is one solid [7zAES, Copy] folder,
since a 7zAES coder sits first and upstream's one-folder-per-file COPY layout keys on coder 0.

